From 3cf9ffff6887dc417d02d228cfc017c9c14cf60f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kudbee <twd4lifez@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 13:45:41 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] feat(security): whole-dashboard sign-in +
 backend-authoritative UpCloud exec policy (#286)

* feat(security): whole-dashboard sign-in + backend-authoritative UpCloud exec policy

Part A: sign-in now covers every /api route except health and auth, plus
the WebSocket upgrade, which is refused before any session exists. Public
health is trimmed to {status, ready}. The dashboard starts nothing until
sign-in, and the kudbee CLI signs in and sends the session cookie.

Part B: new thinkbox/remote_exec_policy.py (upcloud-ssh-readonly v1).
shell:upcloud-ssh:readonly is bound to upcloud-ssh in both directions,
with an exact-match set of six read-only commands. It is enforced at the
route (after admission, before any job) and in
execute_governed_job_command, below the run, resume and reclaim paths.

This closes two gaps. The resume endpoint accepted a caller-supplied
exec_command without governance. AdmissionGate never checked
token.capabilities; it now denies with token_capability_not_granted. The
dashboard token grants only the narrow capability, and jobs record
non-secret execution_policy metadata.

Live, on local loopback (real backend, web server, headless Chrome,
worker-02): signed-out routes and the WebSocket got 401; forbidden
commands got 403 with 0 SSH; the signed-in /remote hostname returned
"kudbee-hermes-worker-02". Tests: backend security 35/35, web 155/155,
typecheck 0, all 5 guards mutation-checked. CI not run (GitHub billing
issue).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

* fix(security): check identity capability before token scope in AdmissionGate

The governance regression (94 modules, 1755 tests) found 2 failures. The
new token-scope check ran before the identity check, which changed the
established capability_not_granted reason when a capability is missing
from both. Identity is now checked first and token scope second, so
token_capability_not_granted fires only in the case it targets: the
identity holds the capability but the token does not. The affected set
re-ran 39/39, and the live backend policy checks were re-run with the
current code.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

